From: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 21:19:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Nest: Fix use-after-free when reconfiguring a protocol that is down

Triggered by `configure` with a config that enables, changes or removes
a protocol which is currently down (e.g. disabled) and runs in the main
loop, i.e. any protocol without its own loop like kernel, device,
direct or static, and the parent of dynamic BGP. Example: remove
`disabled;` from `protocol static s1 { disabled; ... }` and run
`birdc configure` ("Enabling protocol s1").

For such a protocol, proto_rethink_goal() calls proto_cleanup() right
away, which frees struct proto together with its persistent pool and
creates the replacement instance. protos_do_commit() then passes the
freed protocol to proto_announce_state(). ASan reports a heap
use-after-free in protos_do_commit(); with musl, bird may crash on
configure.

Skip the announcement when the protocol is cleaned up synchronously;
proto_cleanup() has already announced its removal.
---
--- a/nest/proto.c
+++ b/nest/proto.c
@@ -2094,9 +2094,14 @@ protos_do_commit(struct config *new, struct config *old, int type)
       proto_rethink_goal_pending++;
 
       p->reconfiguring = PC_SHUTDOWN;
+
+      /* A protocol which is down in the main loop gets cleaned up
+       * and freed by proto_rethink_goal() right away */
+      bool freed = (p->loop == &main_birdloop) && (p->proto_state == PS_DOWN_XX);
       proto_rethink_goal(p);
 
-      proto_announce_state(p, p->ea_state);
+      if (!freed)
+	proto_announce_state(p, p->ea_state);
       PROTO_LEAVE_FROM_MAIN(proto_loop);
     }
   }
